**Weaknesses 2:The author in Theorem 2 proposes a mapping from Euclidean space to hyperbolic space and claims it can preserves direction and local semantics. However, I believe here is a just heuristic operation. The author need to provide a proof of Riemannian geometric consistency, and error bounds to support this claims.**

Our Euclidean–hyperbolic mapping $\phi(s)$ is not a heuristic operation. It is built directly from the Riemannian exponential map on the Poincaré ball and its conformal property. In Appendix C, Eqs. 30–35, we prove that $\phi(s)$ can always be written in radial form $\phi(s) = \alpha(\|s\|)s,$ for a scalar function $\alpha(\cdot)$. Hence $\phi(s)$ and $s$ have exactly the same direction for all $s \neq 0$. In the same part of the appendix we also show that
\begin{equation}
  \lim_{\|s\|\to 0} \frac{\|\phi(s)\|}{\|s\|} = 1,
\end{equation}
so in a neighbourhood of the origin the mapping preserves lengths to first order.

We work on the Poincaré ball $\mathbb D^d$ with metric
\begin{equation}
  G(x) = \lambda_x^2 I, \qquad \lambda_x = \frac{2}{1-\|x\|^2},
\end{equation}

which is the standard hyperbolic metric of constant curvature $-1$ [1,2]. The Poincaré ball is conformal, so it preserves angles at every point [3,4]. In this setting, Eq. 26 defines the exponential map at the centre $0 \in \mathbb D^d$ as
\begin{equation}
  \exp_0(s) = \tanh(\|s\|)\frac{s}{\|s\|}, \quad s \in T_0\mathbb D^d \cong \mathbb R^d,
\end{equation}
and $\phi(s)$ is obtained from $\exp_0(s)$ by the scaling described in Appendix C. This is exactly the construction used in Theorem 2.

From $\phi(s) = \alpha(\|s\|)s$ and 
$\|\phi(s)\|/\|s\| \to 1$ as $\|s\|\to 0$, there exists a radius $\rho>0$ and a constant $C_1>0$ such that for all $\|s\|\le\rho$,
\begin{equation}
  \bigl|\|\phi(s)\| - \|s\|\bigr| \le C_1 \|s\|^2.
\end{equation}
Thus, in the region where we apply the mapping, the distortion of the Euclidean norm is at most quadratic in $\|s\|$. 

For the hyperbolic distance, on the Poincaré ball we have
\begin{equation}
  d_{\mathbb D}(0,x) = 2\operatorname{arctanh}(\|x\|).
\end{equation}
Setting $x = \phi(s)$ and using that $\|\phi(s)\|/\|s\|\to 1$, the same argument gives a radius $\rho>0$ and a constant $C_2>0$ such that for all $\|s\|\le\rho$,
\begin{equation}
  \bigl|d_{\mathbb D}(0,\phi(s)) - \|s\|\bigr| \le C_2 \|s\|^2.
\end{equation}
This, along with the exact preservation of direction, demonstrates that $\phi(s)$ maintains direction and local semantics within a controlled quadratic error.

[1] Foundations of Hyperbolic Manifolds. Springer, 2006

[2] Metric Spaces of Non-Positive Curvature. Springer, 2011

[3] Cannon et al.,  Hyperbolic geometry. volume 31 of MSRI Publications, pp. 59–115.

[4] Poincaré embeddings for learning hierarchical representations., NeurIPS 2017

**Weaknesses 3:The Möbius-addition in narrative fusion provides neither curvature-control conditions nor a convergence analysis, so the claim of “preserving geometric consistency” is unspported.**

The embedding $\tilde q_i$ produced by M\"obius fusion exists in the same hyperbolic space as the sub-question embedding $q_i^*$ and the story embedding $s_D$. In Theorem 3, Eq. 10, the fusion step is

\begin{equation}
\tilde q_i = q_i^* \oplus_{\mathbb D} s_D =\frac{(1+\langle q_i^*, s_D\rangle)q_i^\* + (1 - \|q_i^\*\|^2)s_D}{1 + 2\langle q_i^\*, s_D\rangle + \|q_i^\*\|^2 \|s_D\|^2},
\end{equation}

All three embeddings lie in the same Poincar\'e ball
\begin{equation}
\mathbb D^d = \{x \in \mathbb R^d : \|x\| < 1\}, \qquad G(x) = \lambda_x^2 I,\quad \lambda_x = \frac{2}{1-\|x\|^2},
\end{equation}
and this metric has constant curvature $-1$. From Theorem 2 we know that $q_i^\*$ and $s_D$ both satisfy $\|q_i^*\| < 1$ and $\|s_D\| < 1$. Using the inequality $|\langle q_i^\*, s_D\rangle| \le \|q_i^\*\|\,\|s_D\|$ in Eq. 10, we can bound the norm of the fused point, 
\begin{equation}
\|\tilde q_i\|\le\frac{\|q_i^\*\| + \|s_D\|}{1 + \|q_i^\*\|\,\|s_D\|}< 1.
\end{equation}

Thus $\tilde q_i$ also remains inside $\mathbb D^d$ and inherits the same metric $G(\tilde q_i)$ and the same curvature $-1$. M\"obius fusion does not move the embedding outside the hyperbolic manifold; it produces a new point that stays in the same constant-curvature space as $q_i^*$ and $s_D$, which is the geometric consistency we aim to preserve in this step.

We would like to thank the reviewer for the helpful and constructive comments. We have tried our best to address your concerns. We have included all the analyses, discussions, and experimental results presented in this rebuttal in the revised submission.

**Weaknesses 1: However, the Hamiltonian system in this paper appears to be only a formal analogy, there is no guarantee that the semantic evolution can actually be described or integrated under a symplectic structure. How do the authors justify that the language representation space satisfies the assumptions of a Hamiltonian system?**

Our Hamiltonian system is not a formal analogy. It ensures that the semantic evolution is described and integrated within a real symplectic structure. On any smooth manifold $\mathcal M$, the cotangent bundle $T^*\mathcal M$ carries the canonical symplectic form $\omega=d\theta$, which is induced by the Liouville one-form $\theta$ [1,2]. We embed each sub-question as $q_i \in \mathbb{D}^n$ on the Poincaré ball. We use $v_i \in \mathbb{D}^n$ as the semantic direction extracted in preprocessing and define the momentum with the hyperbolic log map $p_i = \log_{q_i}(v_i) \in T_{q_i} \mathbb{D}^n$. We set the Hamiltonian in Eq.5. The kinetic term matches the Poincaré metric. The potential $U(q)$ is designed to align semantics with the extracted direction while ensuring temporal and order consistency across sub-questions. This construction allows the semantic trajectory to be described and integrated within a real symplectic structure. For optimization, we use a symmetric symplectic integrator, as detailed in the Methods section (Eq. 8 and Algorithm 1). This ensures that each update stays on the manifold and preserves the symplectic structure, which prevents drift over multiple steps.

[1] V.I. Arnold. Mathematical methods of classical mechanics, volume 60. Springer, 1989.

[2] Introduction to Mechanics and Symmetry: A Basic Exposition of Classical Mechanical Systems. Springer , 2013.

**Proof**
We work on the cotangent bundle $T^* \mathbb{D}^n$ of the Poincaré ball $\mathbb D^n$.  
Let the Liouville one-form be $\theta=\sum_j p_j\,dq^j$ and the canonical symplectic form be $\omega=d\theta=\sum_j dq^j\wedge dp_j$.  
The Hamiltonian used in the paper is
\begin{equation}
H(q_i,p_i)=\frac{(1-\|q_i\|^2)^2}{8}\Big\|\frac{2}{\lambda_{q_i}}\tanh^{-1}\big(\|{-}q_i\oplus v_i\|\big)\frac{{-}q_i\oplus v_i}{\{-}q_i\oplus v_i\|}\Big\|^2+U(q_i) ,                \qquad (a)
\end{equation}
where $\lambda_{q_i}=2/(1-\|q_i\|^2)$ and $\oplus$ denotes Möbius addition.
By the hyperbolic log map,
\begin{equation}
p_i=\log_{q_i}(v_i)=\frac{2}{\lambda_{q_i}}\tanh^{-1}\big(\|{-}q_i\oplus v_i\|\big)\frac{{-}q_i\oplus v_i}{\|{-}q_i\oplus v_i\|}\in T_{q_i}\mathbb D^n,\qquad 
\end{equation}
so the norm in Eq.(a) is $\|p_i\|$.
The Poincaré metric is $G(q_i)=\lambda_{q_i}^{\,2}I$, hence $G(q_i)^{-1}=\tfrac{(1-\|q_i\|^2)^2}{4}I$.
Substituting gives
\begin{equation}
H(q_i,p_i)=\tfrac12\,p_i^\top G(q_i)^{-1}p_i+U(q_i).\qquad 
\end{equation}

Let $X_H$ satisfy $\iota_{X_H}\omega=dH$. In local Darboux coordinates $(q,p)$ on $T^*\mathbb D^n$, Eq.(a) yields the Hamilton equations

\begin{equation}
\dot q=\frac{\partial H}{\partial p}=G(q)^{-1}p,\qquad \dot p=-\frac{\partial H}{\partial q} = -\nabla_q\Big(\tfrac12\,p^\top G(q)^{-1}p\Big)-\nabla_q U(q).
\end{equation}
Cartan’s identity gives
\begin{equation}
\mathcal L_{X_H}\omega
=d(\iota_{X_H}\omega)+\iota_{X_H}(d\omega)
=d(dH)=0,
\end{equation}

so the continuous flow $\Phi^t$ preserves $\omega$, i.e., $(\Phi^t)^*\omega=\omega$. 

Hence the trajectory $(q(t),p(t))$ produced by Eq.(a) evolves within the symplectic structure on $T^*\mathbb D^n$.

For the discrete update in Algorithm 1, let the step size be $h$.
Define the Riemannian midpoint $\bar q$ and midpoint momentum $\bar p$ by

\begin{equation}
\bar q=\operatorname{Exp}_{q_i}\Big(\tfrac12\operatorname{Log}\_{q_i}(q\_{i+1})\Big),\qquad \bar p=\tfrac12\Big(\mathsf P\_{q_i\to \bar q}p_i+\mathsf P\_{q\_{i+1}\to \bar q}p\_{i+1}\Big),
\end{equation}

where $\operatorname{Exp},\operatorname{Log}$ are the Poincaré maps and $\mathsf P$ is the parallel transport along the geodesic.

The implemented midpoint step is
\begin{equation}
\operatorname{Log}\_{\bar q}(q\_{i+1})=hG(\bar q)^{-1}\bar p,\qquad \mathsf P\_{\bar q\to q\_{i+1}}\bar p=\mathsf P\_{q_i\to \bar q}p_i-h\,\nabla_q U(\bar q).
\end{equation}

In canonical coordinates $z=(q,p)$ this corresponds to the one-stage Gauss--Legendre (implicit midpoint) map
\begin{equation}
z_{i+1}=z_i+h\,J\,\nabla H\!\Big(\tfrac{z_i+z_{i+1}}{2}\Big),\qquad 
J=\begin{pmatrix}0&I\\\\-I&0\end{pmatrix}.
\end{equation}

The implicit midpoint method is symplectic, satisfying $(D\Psi_h)^\top J\,D\Psi_h=J$, so the discrete map $\Psi_h$ preserves $\omega$.

Consequently, the update used in Algorithm 1 respects the symplectic geometry defined by Eqs. 5-6, ensuring that each optimization step remains within the symplectic structure.
